  "theory": {
        "intro": "The distributive law works with surds exactly as it does with pronumerals. Expand first, then simplify every surd and collect any like surds. Watch for $\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{a}=a$, which collapses surd terms into whole numbers part-way through.",
        "facts": [
          "**Single bracket:** $a\\left(b+c\\right)=ab+ac$, so $\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{5}\\right)=\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{10}$.",
          "**Binomial product:** $\\left(a+b\\right)\\left(c+d\\right)=ac+ad+bc+bd$ — every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second.",
          "**Perfect square:** $\\left(a+b\\right)^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}$ and $\\left(a-b\\right)^{2}=a^{2}-2ab+b^{2}$; the middle term is the one most often lost.",
          "**Difference of two squares:** $\\left(a+b\\right)\\left(a-b\\right)=a^{2}-b^{2}$, so $\\left(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{2}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ has **no surds** in its answer.",
          "$\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{a}=a$, so $\\left(2\\sqrt{3}\\right)^{2}=4\\times 3=12$."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Expand the brackets",
          "Simplify each surd",
          "Collect like terms"
        ]
      },
